The compatibility of MIL-53(Al,Fe) with \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}\) underscores two important distinctions between biomimetic MOFs and the enzymes they emulate. First, the key active species can be accessed with reagents and conditions that are too harsh for most enzymatic catalysis. Secondly, the high thermal and chemical stability of many MOFs can be exploited to augment the reactivity of biologically inspired intermediates beyond that of the native enzymes. In 2014 Long and coworkers similarly leveraged both of these properties to effect the oxidation of ethane using \(\mathsf{F e}_{0.1}\mathsf{M g}_{1.9}(\mathsf{d o b d c})\) , a bimetallic MOF-74 analog. Treatment of this material with \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) (an oxygen atom transfer reagent) at elevated temperatures resulted in the formation of
a reactive intermediate capable of oxidizing ethane.52,53 DFT calculations implicate a high-spin \(\mathsf{S}=2\), \(\mathsf{F e}_{0}(\mathsf{I V})\mathsf{O}\) species as the active oxidant, which is similar to those proposed for many mononuclear non-heme Fe oxidases.54,55 In contrast to the diiron active site of sMMO, mononuclear non-heme oxidases are not generally thought to be competent in the oxidation of strong \(\mathsf{C{-}H}\) bonds of ethane or methane. The observed oxidation of ethane by the putative \(\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{I V}){=}0\) species is likely enabled, at least in part, by the high thermal and chemical stability of \(\mathsf{F e}_{0.1}\mathsf{M g}_{1.9}(\mathsf{d o b d c})\).53
## Oxygen
Dioxygen is a thermodynamically potent oxidant that drives nearly all metabolic processes in aerobic organisms. As a resonance stabilized ground-state triplet, it is kinetically sluggish in the oxidation of singlet substrates.56 Consequently, most synthetically important transformations involving \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) activation require enzymes bearing open-shell transition metals such as Fe or \(\mathsf{C u}\).57,58 Key questions still remain over how these enzymes convert kinetically stable molecular \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) into strongly oxidizing metal—oxygen species (e.g. M—oxo/oxyls, M—hydroperoxos, etc.) or how subtle changes in ligand field perturb elementary aspects of \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) activation. SBU-based Fe and Cu centers provide opportunities to probe detailed features of \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) activation that underlie some of the most remarkable transformations catalyzed by \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\)-dependent oxidases. The permanent porosity and crystallinity of MOFs enable investigation of \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) binding through gas sorption studies and crystal-to-crystal transformations. This section highlights select examples of \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) binding and activation at metal sites bearing ligand fields similar to those found in important \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\)-activating metalloenzymes.Some of the earliest steps in studying detailed aspects of \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) activation with MOFs were reported in 2011 by Long and coworkers, who characterized the temperature-dependent \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) bonding to the open metal sites in \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}(\mathsf{d o b d c})\) (Fe-MOF-74, \(\mathsf{d o b d c}^{4-}=2\),5-dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate).59 At 298 K, approximately half of the Fe(II) sites bind \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) irreversibly. Lowering the temperature to 211 K results in reversible \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) binding and full occupancy of the available Fe(II) sites. The difference in bonding mode at variable temperature was further evidenced by neutron diffraction studies under an \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) atmosphere at 94 K and 220 K (Figure 4). At 94 K, partial electron transfer from the \(\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I}}\) site to \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) generates \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}(\mathsf{O}_{2})_{2}(\mathsf{d o b d c})\). Warming this material to 220 K results in formal electron transfer from Fe to \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) to produce a high-spin side-on \(\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I I}}\)-peroxide, as supported by Mössbauer and IR spectroscopy, as well as Rietveld refinement of neutron diffraction data. Although this study was originally described in the context of gas separations, these observations are relevant to the chemistry of non-haem diiron oxygenases. The SBU of Fe-MOF-74 enforces a comparable Fe–Fe distance and an all-oxygen ligand field similar to that of oxygenases.42 The exact nature of \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) activation in these enzymes is not completely understood.60 Related studies of \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) activation by other diiron
motifs in MOFs may shed new light on the mechanisms through which diiron oxidases function.
